Patch summary:
The GLProcessPipe service gives virtio-gpu guests the invalid process id
again, as it did before upstream re-implemented the pipes (44977c960e). The
guest sends that id back with rcSetPuid(), which ignores only the invalid
one. With a real id every render thread moved from its virtio-gpu context id
to the pipe's id, and with it the GL contexts, window surfaces and EGLImages
it made and the Vulkan context id that instance cleanup and exported syncs
are registered under. virtio-gpu cleans a dead guest process up by context
id, so none of that was freed when the app died.
diff --git a/host/virtio_gpu_pipe.cpp b/host/virtio_gpu_pipe.cpp
index 67a1e72..9b121fe 100644
--- a/host/virtio_gpu_pipe.cpp
+++ b/host/virtio_gpu_pipe.cpp
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 
 #include <atomic>
 #include <cstring>
+#include <limits>
 
 #include "gfxstream/common/logging.h"
 #include "gfxstream/system/System.h"
@@ -90,7 +91,12 @@ int VirtioGpuProcessPipe::TransferFromHost(char* outRequestedData, size_t reques
         return -EINVAL;
     }
 
-    std::memcpy(outRequestedData, (const char*)&mUniqueId, sizeof(mUniqueId));
+    // The guest hands this id back with rcSetPuid(), and the render thread would then own its
+    // GL contexts, surfaces and images under it. virtio-gpu cleans a guest process up by its
+    // context id instead, so send the invalid id, which rcSetPuid() ignores, as the
+    // GLProcessPipe this replaced did: otherwise nothing a guest app made was freed when it died.
+    const uint64_t invalidPuid = std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max();
+    std::memcpy(outRequestedData, (const char*)&invalidPuid, sizeof(invalidPuid));
 
     mSentUniqueId = true;
     return 0;
